      {
       "id": "CVE-2026-108106",
       "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
       "shortDescription": {
        "text": "CVE-2026-108106: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling"
       },
       "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-108106?s=github&n=snappy-java&ns=org.xerial.snappy&t=maven&vr=%3C%3D1.1.10.8",
       "help": {
        "text": "### Impact\nSeveral decompression paths in snappy-java allocate an output buffer sized by the uncompressed length declared in the first bytes of the compressed data, before decompressing or validating it. A few bytes of crafted input can therefore force an allocation of up to 2 GB, causing `OutOfMemoryError` (denial of service), or a `NegativeArraySizeException` for declared lengths of 2^31 or more.\n\nAffected entry points include `Snappy.uncompress(byte[])`, `Snappy.uncompressString`, the `Snappy.uncompress*Array` methods, `SnappyInputStream`, and `SnappyFramedInputStream`. The `maxChunkSize` limit added for CVE-2023-43642 bounds only the compressed chunk length, so it does not prevent this.\n\n### Patches\nFixed in 1.1.10.9 (#739). `Snappy.uncompressedLength()` now rejects negative declared lengths, and lengths larger than any valid Snappy stream of the given size can produce. The densest Snappy element expands 3 bytes to 64, so the limit is 64/3 of the compressed size. Allocations are therefore proportional to the input actually received.\n\n### Workarounds\nBefore decompressing untrusted data, check that `Snappy.uncompressedLength(input)` is within an application-specific limit.\n",
        "markdown": "> ### Impact\nSeveral decompression paths in snappy-java allocate an output buffer sized by the uncompressed length declared in the first bytes of the compressed data, before decompressing or validating it. A few bytes of crafted input can therefore force an allocation of up to 2 GB, causing `OutOfMemoryError` (denial of service), or a `NegativeArraySizeException` for declared lengths of 2^31 or more.\n\nAffected entry points include `Snappy.uncompress(byte[])`, `Snappy.uncompressString`, the `Snappy.uncompress*Array` methods, `SnappyInputStream`, and `SnappyFramedInputStream`. The `maxChunkSize` limit added for CVE-2023-43642 bounds only the compressed chunk length, so it does not prevent this.\n\n### Patches\nFixed in 1.1.10.9 (#739). `Snappy.uncompressedLength()` now rejects negative declared lengths, and lengths larger than any valid Snappy stream of the given size can produce. The densest Snappy element expands 3 bytes to 64, so the limit is 64/3 of the compressed size. Allocations are therefore proportional to the input actually received.\n\n### Workarounds\nBefore decompressing untrusted data, check that `Snappy.uncompressedLength(input)` is within an application-specific limit.\n\n|                                                                                                                                 |\n|Package                                                                        pkg:maven/org.xerial.snappy/snappy-java@1.1.10.7  |\n|Affected range                                                                 <=1.1.10.8                                        |\n|Fixed version                                                                  1.1.10.9                                          |\n|[[CVSS Score 7.5] [CVSS Vector CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H]]                                                    |\n"
       },
       "properties": {
        "affected_version": "<=1.1.10.8",
        "cvssV3": 7.5,
        "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
        "cvssV3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H",
        "fixed_version": "1.1.10.9",
