      {
       "id": "CVE-2026-102554",
       "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
       "shortDescription": {
        "text": "CVE-2026-102554: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption"
       },
       "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-102554?s=github&n=guava&ns=com.google.guava&t=maven&vr=%3E%3D4.0%2C%3C%3D33.7.1",
       "help": {
        "text": "# CompactHashMap/CompactHashSet/MapMakerInternalMap native deserialization triggers uncontrolled memory allocation\n\n## Summary\n\n`CompactHashMap`, `CompactHashSet`, and `MapMakerInternalMap` implement `readObject(ObjectInputStream)` by reading an attacker-controlled `elementCount` or `initialCapacity` from the stream. On the very first insertion during or after deserialization (for the `CompactHash*` collections, inside `allocArrays()`) or immediately during deserialization (for `MapMakerInternalMap`), the implementation eagerly allocates arrays sized to that full, unvalidated value (up to `CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE` = 2^30-1, ~1.07 billion, for example) before the rest of the stream's actual content has been consumed. A 72-byte malicious serialized stream containing a single tiny element can force a multi-gigabyte allocation and crash a JVM with `OutOfMemoryError`, dynamically confirmed against the current release.\n\n## Details\n\n`CompactHashMap.readObject()` (`guava/src/com/google/common/collect/CompactHashMap.java`):\n\n```java\nprivate void readObject(ObjectInputStream stream) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {\n  stream.defaultReadObject();\n  int elementCount = stream.readInt();\n  if (elementCount < 0) {\n    throw new InvalidObjectException(\"Invalid size: \" + elementCount);\n  }\n  init(elementCount);\n  for (int i = 0; i < elementCount; i++) {\n    K key = (K) stream.readObject();\n    V value = (V) stream.readObject();\n    put(key, value);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`elementCount` is only checked for being non-negative; there is no upper bound and no relationship enforced between the declared count and how much data actually follows in the stream. `init(elementCount)` (line 251) just stores the value (clamped to `[1, CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE]`, i.e. up to 2^30-1):\n\n```java\nvoid init(int expectedSize) {\n  Preconditions.checkArgument(expectedSize >= 0, \"Expected size must be >= 0\");\n  this.metadata = Ints.constrainToRange(expectedSize, 1, CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE);\n}\n```\n\nThe real allocation is deferred to `allocArrays()`, which fires lazily on the *first* `put()` call \u2014 i.e. after only one key/value pair has actually been read off the wire:\n\n```java\nint allocArrays() {\n  ...\n  int expectedSize = metadata;\n  int buckets = CompactHashing.tableSize(expectedSize);\n  this.table = CompactHashing.createTable(buckets);\n  setHashTableMask(buckets - 1);\n  this.entries = new int[expectedSize];\n  this.keys = new Object[expectedSize];\n  this.values = new Object[expectedSize];\n  return expectedSize;\n}\n```\n\nWith `expectedSize` near `CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE` (2^30-1), this single call allocates a hash `table` plus three `expectedSize`-length arrays \u2014 multiple gigabytes \u2014 triggered by a stream that has, at that point, only supplied one small real element. `CompactHashSet.readObject()`/`allocArrays()` have the identical shape. `CompactLinkedHashMap`/`CompactLinkedHashSet` extend these classes and inherit the same unguarded path.\n\nThe problem with `MapMakerInternalMap` is similar, with [a size read from the serialized data and stored in a `MapMaker` instance](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2848-L2851) for [later user during deserialization](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2897), when [the `MapMaker` is passed back to `MapMakerInternalMap`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMaker.java#L287) and [segments are allocated](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L173-L200) by [calling `newSegment`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1143), whose implementations ([example](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L443)) create `Segment` instances ([example](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2019)) that [allocate arrays of the requested capacity](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1234) through [the `newTable`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1262) helper.\n\nThis is the same amplification shape as the already-fixed **CVE-2018-10237** (`AtomicDoubleArray`/`CompoundOrdering`: attacker declares a large size, library eagerly allocates before validating against actual stream content). That fix rewrote `AtomicDoubleArray.readObject()` to grow an `ImmutableLongArray.Builder` incrementally per element actually read, rather than pre-allocating from the declared size \u2014 Guava's own `HashBiMap.readObject()` documents the same defensive principle explicitly in a comment: `init(16); // resist hostile attempts to allocate gratuitous heap`. `CompactHashMap`/`CompactHashSet` do not follow either of these two patterns already present elsewhere in the same codebase.\n\n**Reachability**: All classes are top-level, `implements ... Serializable`, and require no special classpath setup beyond Guava being present (near-ubiquitous on JVM backends). A native Java deserialization attacker names the class directly in a hand-crafted stream \u2014 no particular Guava factory method needs to have been used by the victim application, exactly as with the precedent CVE.\n\n## PoC\n\nFull source and run instructions in the attached `poc-guava-compacthash-dos.zip` (Maven project, `guava:33.7.1-jre` \u2014 the current latest release \u2014 as the only dependency). The PoC:\n\n1. Uses reflection only to legitimately obtain a `CompactHashSet` instance (it is package-private; a real attacker does not need this step, since they craft the serialized bytes directly rather than calling Guava's own serialization code).\n2. Serializes one set with 1 element and one with 2 elements, diffs the two byte streams to locate the exact offset of the `elementCount` field (byte-precise, not guessed).\n3. Patches that one 4-byte field in the 1-element stream from `1` to `0x3FFFFFFF` (`CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE`), leaving the rest of the 72-byte stream (one real `\"A\"` element) untouched.\n4. Feeds the resulting 72-byte malicious stream into a plain `new ObjectInputStream(...).readObject()` call against the real, unmodified `guava-33.7.1-jre.jar`.\n\nActual run output:\n\n```\n### -Xmx8g (generous heap) ###\nLocated size field at offset 63, value=1 (expected 1)\nMalicious stream size: 72 bytes\nDeclared elementCount: 1073741823 (~1.07 billion)\nReal payload: 1 tiny string element (\"A\")\n=== RESULT ===\nException: java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space\nTime to allocate+fail: 4013 ms\nHeap used before: 3 MB\nHeap used after (at failure point): 4103 MB\nJVM max heap (-Xmx): 8192 MB\n\n### -Xmx128m (ordinary service-sized heap) ###\nLocated size field at offset 63, value=1 (expected 1)\nMalicious stream size: 72 bytes\nDeclared elementCount: 1073741823 (~1.07 billion)\nReal payload: 1 tiny string element (\"A\")\n=== RESULT ===\nException: java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space\nTime to allocate+fail: 24 ms\nHeap used before: 4 MB\nHeap used after (at failure point): 2 MB\nJVM max heap (-Xmx): 128 MB\n```\n\nA 72-byte input forces ~4.1 GB of real allocation before failing on an 8 GB heap, and crashes a 128 MB heap in 24 ms \u2014 over 50,000x amplification between input size and forced allocation.\n\nTo reproduce: `mvn -q compile dependency:build-classpath -Dmdep.outputFile=cp.txt && java -Xmx128m -cp \"target/classes:$(cat cp.txt)\" poc.Poc`\n\n## Impact\n\nUncontrolled resource consumption (CWE-770 / CWE-400) via native Java deserialization: any application that runs `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` on data reachable by an attacker, and has Guava on its classpath (true for a large fraction of the JVM ecosystem), can be crashed with a single ~72-byte payload, regardless of what type the application itself expected to deserialize.\n\n## Severity\n\nCVSS 3.1: `AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H` \u2014 identical vector to CVE-2018-10237, the precedent this is a sibling of. Recomputed with the full formula: exploitability = 8.22\u00d70.85\u00d70.44\u00d70.85\u00d70.85 = 2.221; ISS = 1-(1\u00d71\u00d70.44) = 0.56; impact = 6.42\u00d70.56 = 3.595; base = roundup(5.816) = **5.9 (Medium)**.\n\n## Affected\n\n`com.google.guava:guava`, all editions (`-jre`/`-android`) \u2014 confirmed on the current latest release, 33.7.1-jre. `CompactHashMap`/`CompactHashSet`/`CompactLinkedHashMap`/`CompactLinkedHashSet` have had this same `readObject`/`allocArrays` shape for a long time (probably since they were added in Guava 23.6), and similarly for `MapMakerInternalMap` (which was introduced in Guava 4.0); not tested against older individual releases.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nApply the same defense already used elsewhere in this codebase: either clamp the initial `init()` capacity to a small constant regardless of the declared `elementCount` (as `HashBiMap.readObject()` already does \u2014 `init(16); // resist hostile attempts to allocate gratuitous heap`) and grow incrementally as elements are actually read, or grow the backing arrays incrementally per iteration of the deserialization loop (as the fixed `AtomicDoubleArray.readObject()` does via `ImmutableLongArray.Builder`) instead of eagerly sizing to the full attacker-declared count on first insert.\n",
        "markdown": "> # CompactHashMap/CompactHashSet/MapMakerInternalMap native deserialization triggers uncontrolled memory allocation\n\n## Summary\n\n`CompactHashMap`, `CompactHashSet`, and `MapMakerInternalMap` implement `readObject(ObjectInputStream)` by reading an attacker-controlled `elementCount` or `initialCapacity` from the stream. On the very first insertion during or after deserialization (for the `CompactHash*` collections, inside `allocArrays()`) or immediately during deserialization (for `MapMakerInternalMap`), the implementation eagerly allocates arrays sized to that full, unvalidated value (up to `CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE` = 2^30-1, ~1.07 billion, for example) before the rest of the stream's actual content has been consumed. A 72-byte malicious serialized stream containing a single tiny element can force a multi-gigabyte allocation and crash a JVM with `OutOfMemoryError`, dynamically confirmed against the current release.\n\n## Details\n\n`CompactHashMap.readObject()` (`guava/src/com/google/common/collect/CompactHashMap.java`):\n\n```java\nprivate void readObject(ObjectInputStream stream) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {\n  stream.defaultReadObject();\n  int elementCount = stream.readInt();\n  if (elementCount < 0) {\n    throw new InvalidObjectException(\"Invalid size: \" + elementCount);\n  }\n  init(elementCount);\n  for (int i = 0; i < elementCount; i++) {\n    K key = (K) stream.readObject();\n    V value = (V) stream.readObject();\n    put(key, value);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`elementCount` is only checked for being non-negative; there is no upper bound and no relationship enforced between the declared count and how much data actually follows in the stream. `init(elementCount)` (line 251) just stores the value (clamped to `[1, CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE]`, i.e. up to 2^30-1):\n\n```java\nvoid init(int expectedSize) {\n  Preconditions.checkArgument(expectedSize >= 0, \"Expected size must be >= 0\");\n  this.metadata = Ints.constrainToRange(expectedSize, 1, CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE);\n}\n```\n\nThe real allocation is deferred to `allocArrays()`, which fires lazily on the *first* `put()` call \u2014 i.e. after only one key/value pair has actually been read off the wire:\n\n```java\nint allocArrays() {\n  ...\n  int expectedSize = metadata;\n  int buckets = CompactHashing.tableSize(expectedSize);\n  this.table = CompactHashing.createTable(buckets);\n  setHashTableMask(buckets - 1);\n  this.entries = new int[expectedSize];\n  this.keys = new Object[expectedSize];\n  this.values = new Object[expectedSize];\n  return expectedSize;\n}\n```\n\nWith `expectedSize` near `CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE` (2^30-1), this single call allocates a hash `table` plus three `expectedSize`-length arrays \u2014 multiple gigabytes \u2014 triggered by a stream that has, at that point, only supplied one small real element. `CompactHashSet.readObject()`/`allocArrays()` have the identical shape. `CompactLinkedHashMap`/`CompactLinkedHashSet` extend these classes and inherit the same unguarded path.\n\nThe problem with `MapMakerInternalMap` is similar, with [a size read from the serialized data and stored in a `MapMaker` instance](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2848-L2851) for [later user during deserialization](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2897), when [the `MapMaker` is passed back to `MapMakerInternalMap`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMaker.java#L287) and [segments are allocated](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L173-L200) by [calling `newSegment`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1143), whose implementations ([example](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L443)) create `Segment` instances ([example](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2019)) that [allocate arrays of the requested capacity](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1234) through [the `newTable`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1262) helper.\n\nThis is the same amplification shape as the already-fixed **CVE-2018-10237** (`AtomicDoubleArray`/`CompoundOrdering`: attacker declares a large size, library eagerly allocates before validating against actual stream content). That fix rewrote `AtomicDoubleArray.readObject()` to grow an `ImmutableLongArray.Builder` incrementally per element actually read, rather than pre-allocating from the declared size \u2014 Guava's own `HashBiMap.readObject()` documents the same defensive principle explicitly in a comment: `init(16); // resist hostile attempts to allocate gratuitous heap`. `CompactHashMap`/`CompactHashSet` do not follow either of these two patterns already present elsewhere in the same codebase.\n\n**Reachability**: All classes are top-level, `implements ... Serializable`, and require no special classpath setup beyond Guava being present (near-ubiquitous on JVM backends). A native Java deserialization attacker names the class directly in a hand-crafted stream \u2014 no particular Guava factory method needs to have been used by the victim application, exactly as with the precedent CVE.\n\n## PoC\n\nFull source and run instructions in the attached `poc-guava-compacthash-dos.zip` (Maven project, `guava:33.7.1-jre` \u2014 the current latest release \u2014 as the only dependency). The PoC:\n\n1. Uses reflection only to legitimately obtain a `CompactHashSet` instance (it is package-private; a real attacker does not need this step, since they craft the serialized bytes directly rather than calling Guava's own serialization code).\n2. Serializes one set with 1 element and one with 2 elements, diffs the two byte streams to locate the exact offset of the `elementCount` field (byte-precise, not guessed).\n3. Patches that one 4-byte field in the 1-element stream from `1` to `0x3FFFFFFF` (`CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE`), leaving the rest of the 72-byte stream (one real `\"A\"` element) untouched.\n4. Feeds the resulting 72-byte malicious stream into a plain `new ObjectInputStream(...).readObject()` call against the real, unmodified `guava-33.7.1-jre.jar`.\n\nActual run output:\n\n```\n### -Xmx8g (generous heap) ###\nLocated size field at offset 63, value=1 (expected 1)\nMalicious stream size: 72 bytes\nDeclared elementCount: 1073741823 (~1.07 billion)\nReal payload: 1 tiny string element (\"A\")\n=== RESULT ===\nException: java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space\nTime to allocate+fail: 4013 ms\nHeap used before: 3 MB\nHeap used after (at failure point): 4103 MB\nJVM max heap (-Xmx): 8192 MB\n\n### -Xmx128m (ordinary service-sized heap) ###\nLocated size field at offset 63, value=1 (expected 1)\nMalicious stream size: 72 bytes\nDeclared elementCount: 1073741823 (~1.07 billion)\nReal payload: 1 tiny string element (\"A\")\n=== RESULT ===\nException: java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space\nTime to allocate+fail: 24 ms\nHeap used before: 4 MB\nHeap used after (at failure point): 2 MB\nJVM max heap (-Xmx): 128 MB\n```\n\nA 72-byte input forces ~4.1 GB of real allocation before failing on an 8 GB heap, and crashes a 128 MB heap in 24 ms \u2014 over 50,000x amplification between input size and forced allocation.\n\nTo reproduce: `mvn -q compile dependency:build-classpath -Dmdep.outputFile=cp.txt && java -Xmx128m -cp \"target/classes:$(cat cp.txt)\" poc.Poc`\n\n## Impact\n\nUncontrolled resource consumption (CWE-770 / CWE-400) via native Java deserialization: any application that runs `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` on data reachable by an attacker, and has Guava on its classpath (true for a large fraction of the JVM ecosystem), can be crashed with a single ~72-byte payload, regardless of what type the application itself expected to deserialize.\n\n## Severity\n\nCVSS 3.1: `AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H` \u2014 identical vector to CVE-2018-10237, the precedent this is a sibling of. Recomputed with the full formula: exploitability = 8.22\u00d70.85\u00d70.44\u00d70.85\u00d70.85 = 2.221; ISS = 1-(1\u00d71\u00d70.44) = 0.56; impact = 6.42\u00d70.56 = 3.595; base = roundup(5.816) = **5.9 (Medium)**.\n\n## Affected\n\n`com.google.guava:guava`, all editions (`-jre`/`-android`) \u2014 confirmed on the current latest release, 33.7.1-jre. `CompactHashMap`/`CompactHashSet`/`CompactLinkedHashMap`/`CompactLinkedHashSet` have had this same `readObject`/`allocArrays` shape for a long time (probably since they were added in Guava 23.6), and similarly for `MapMakerInternalMap` (which was introduced in Guava 4.0); not tested against older individual releases.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nApply the same defense already used elsewhere in this codebase: either clamp the initial `init()` capacity to a small constant regardless of the declared `elementCount` (as `HashBiMap.readObject()` already does \u2014 `init(16); // resist hostile attempts to allocate gratuitous heap`) and grow incrementally as elements are actually read, or grow the backing arrays incrementally per iteration of the deserialization loop (as the fixed `AtomicDoubleArray.readObject()` does via `ImmutableLongArray.Builder`) instead of eagerly sizing to the full attacker-declared count on first insert.\n\n|                                                                                                                                               |\n|Package                                                                                           pkg:maven/com.google.guava/guava@32.1.3-jre  |\n|Affected range                                                                                    >=4.0,<=33.7.1                               |\n|Fixed version                                                                                     33.7.2                                       |\n|[[CVSS Score 8.2] [CVSS Vector CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:P/PR:N/UI:N/VC:N/VI:N/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N]]                                               |\n"
       },
       "properties": {
        "affected_version": ">=4.0,<=33.7.1",
        "cvssV3": 8.2,
        "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
        "cvssV3_vector": "CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:P/PR:N/UI:N/VC:N/VI:N/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N",
        "fixed_version": "33.7.2",
        "purls": [
         "pkg:maven/com.google.guava/guava@32.1.2-jre",
         "pkg:maven/com.google.guava/guava@32.1.3-jre"
        ],
        "security-severity": "8.2",
        "tags": [
         "HIGH"
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "id": "CVE-2026-45674",
       "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
       "shortDescription": {
        "text": "CVE-2026-45674: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity"
       },
       "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-45674?s=github&n=netty-resolver-dns&ns=io.netty&t=maven&vr=%3C%3D4.1.134.Final",
       "help": {
        "text": "### Summary\nNetty's DnsResolveContext fails to validate the origin (bailiwick) of CNAME records in DNS responses.\n\n### Details\nIn `io.netty.resolver.dns.DnsResolveContext#buildAliasMap`, the resolver processes the ANSWER section of a DNS response and blindly caches all CNAME records it finds.\n\nAccording to https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5452#section-6 \n\n```\nCare must be taken to only accept\n   data if it is known that the originator is authoritative for the\n   QNAME or a parent of the QNAME.\n   One very simple way to achieve this is to only accept data if it is\n   part of the domain for which the query was intended.\n```\n\n### Impact\nDNS Cache Poisoning (Bailiwick Bypass). Any application using Netty's DNS resolver is impacted.\n",
        "markdown": "> ### Summary\nNetty's DnsResolveContext fails to validate the origin (bailiwick) of CNAME records in DNS responses.\n\n### Details\nIn `io.netty.resolver.dns.DnsResolveContext#buildAliasMap`, the resolver processes the ANSWER section of a DNS response and blindly caches all CNAME records it finds.\n\nAccording to https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5452#section-6 \n\n```\nCare must be taken to only accept\n   data if it is known that the originator is authoritative for the\n   QNAME or a parent of the QNAME.\n   One very simple way to achieve this is to only accept data if it is\n   part of the domain for which the query was intended.\n```\n\n### Impact\nDNS Cache Poisoning (Bailiwick Bypass). Any application using Netty's DNS resolver is impacted.\n\n|                                                                                                                                    |\n|Package                                                                        pkg:maven/io.netty/netty-resolver-dns@4.1.132.Final  |\n|Affected range                                                                 <=4.1.134.Final                                      |\n|Fixed version                                                                  4.1.135.Final                                        |\n|[[CVSS Score 8.7] [CVSS Vector CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:N]]                                                       |\n"
       },
       "properties": {
        "affected_version": "<=4.1.134.Final",
        "cvssV3": 8.7,
        "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
        "cvssV3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:N",
        "fixed_version": "4.1.135.Final",
        "purls": [
         "pkg:maven/io.netty/netty-resolver-dns@4.1.132.Final"
